Don't worry, I ain't gonna hurt you.

454
01:02:29,113 --> 01:02:30,944
I need you
to drive me on outta here.

455
01:02:31,015 --> 01:02:32,880
[Man gurgling]

456
01:02:54,873 --> 01:02:56,670
[Tires screeching]

457
01:04:51,856 --> 01:04:53,653
[Groans]

458
01:05:05,970 --> 01:05:08,905
[Breathing heavily]

459
01:05:34,165 --> 01:05:37,965
[People laughing]

460
01:05:38,036 --> 01:05:40,300
- Why not?
- I don't know, man.

461
01:05:40,371 --> 01:05:42,703
[Indistinct chattering]

462
01:05:52,683 --> 01:05:54,514
[Groans]

463
01:06:03,995 --> 01:06:06,088
Were you in a car accident?

464
01:06:08,032 --> 01:06:10,398
I'll give you 500 bucks for that coat.

465
01:06:12,103 --> 01:06:13,229
Lemme see the money.

466
01:06:16,274 --> 01:06:17,764
Were you in a car accident?

467
01:06:20,078 --> 01:06:22,478
- Yeah.
- OK, give me the money.

468
01:06:22,547 --> 01:06:26,483
- It's right here. Give me the clothes.
- Let him hold the money.

469
01:06:33,224 --> 01:06:34,782
Give it here.

470
01:06:42,667 --> 01:06:43,793
Gimme that beer too.

471
01:06:46,671 --> 01:06:48,298
How much?

472
01:06:49,040 --> 01:06:50,837
Brian, give him the beer.

473
01:07:37,088 --> 01:07:38,988
[Groans]

474
01:07:56,240 --> 01:07:58,765
[Grunting]

475
01:07:58,843 --> 01:08:01,403
[Coyotes howling]

476
01:08:03,181 --> 01:08:06,082
Ah! [coughs]

477
01:08:06,150 --> 01:08:09,278
[Footsteps approaching]

478
01:08:26,537 --> 01:08:29,802
[Mariachi music]

479
01:08:38,516 --> 01:08:41,747
[Singing in Spanish]

480
01:08:52,196 --> 01:08:54,391
[Music, singing stops]

481
01:08:59,604 --> 01:09:01,196
[Groans]

482
01:09:02,974 --> 01:09:04,464
Medico...

483
01:09:05,710 --> 01:09:07,735
...por favor.

484
01:10:53,851 --> 01:10:56,046
[People shout]

485
01:10:59,957 --> 01:11:03,825
[People speaking Spanish]

486
01:11:11,302 --> 01:11:12,599
Wait up!

487
01:11:15,072 --> 01:11:18,439
[TV]... and I had a 44 inch waist.
My diet specialist got a hold of me,

488
01:11:18,509 --> 01:11:20,670
and that first week,
I lost seven pounds!

489
01:11:20,745 --> 01:11:24,306
Looking back on the diet, it was
relatively easy to lose the weight,

490
01:11:24,382 --> 01:11:28,216
though it did have moments. Sometimes,
it looked as if I gained weight

491
01:11:28,285 --> 01:11:31,721
instead of lost it.
The most difficult part is still ahead.

492
01:11:31,789 --> 01:11:34,155
Keeping weight off
now that we've lost it.

493
01:11:34,225 --> 01:11:36,591
It's natural
for you to gain a few pounds.

494
01:11:36,660 --> 01:11:40,289
But as soon as you do,
make sure you go on...

495
01:11:53,511 --> 01:11:55,342
[exhales deeply]

496
01:12:37,588 --> 01:12:41,820
Here are a few points to remember.
First of all, weigh in every day.

497
01:13:11,021 --> 01:13:13,080
Any word on those vehicles yet?

498
01:13:13,157 --> 01:13:16,183
[Woman] Sheriff, I found out
everything there was to find.

499
01:13:16,260 --> 01:13:20,822
Those vehicles are titled
and registered to deceased people.

500
01:13:20,898 --> 01:13:23,389
The owner of that Bronco's
been dead 20 years.

501
01:13:23,467 --> 01:13:25,697
Want me to find out
about the Mexican ones?

502
01:13:25,769 --> 01:13:28,567
No. Lord, no.

503
01:13:28,639 --> 01:13:30,698
There's this month's checks.

504
01:13:31,242 --> 01:13:34,234
That DEA agent called again.
You don't wanna talk to him?

505
01:13:34,311 --> 01:13:37,007
Gonna try to keep from it much as I can.

506
01:13:37,081 --> 01:13:40,073
Going back. Wanted to know
if you wanted to go with him.

507
01:13:40,150 --> 01:13:41,378
That's cordial of him.

508
01:13:43,320 --> 01:13:44,981
Can I get you to call Loretta,

509
01:13:45,055 --> 01:13:47,751
tell her I'm going to Odessa
to see Carla Jean Moss?

510
01:13:47,825 --> 01:13:50,817
- Yes, sheriff.
- I'll call her when I get there.

511
01:13:50,895 --> 01:13:53,625
I'd call now, but if she
want me to come home, I might.

512
01:13:53,697 --> 01:13:56,495
Do you want me to wait
till you quit the building?

513
01:13:56,567 --> 01:14:00,298
Uh-huh. Don't wanna lie without
what it's absolutely necessary.

514
01:14:03,874 --> 01:14:06,399
What is it Torbert says
about truth and justice?

515
01:14:07,111 --> 01:14:11,104
Oh, we dedicate ourselves daily anew.
Something like that.

516
01:14:11,181 --> 01:14:14,207
I'm gonna commence
dedicating myself twice daily.

517
01:14:14,285 --> 01:14:17,118
- Might come to three before it's over.
- [Tires screech]

518
01:14:18,589 --> 01:14:19,920
What the hell?

519
01:14:20,824 --> 01:14:22,621
[Siren sounds]

520
01:14:28,766 --> 01:14:30,131
Sheriff?

521
01:14:30,668 --> 01:14:32,727
You looked at your load lately?

522
01:14:34,305 --> 01:14:38,833
- That is a damn outrage.
- One of those tie-downs worked loose.

523
01:14:38,909 --> 01:14:44,211
- How many bodies did you leave with?
- I ain't lost none of 'em, sheriff.

524
01:14:44,281 --> 01:14:46,647
Couldn't y'all have
taken a van out there?

525
01:14:46,717 --> 01:14:49,083
We didn't have no van
with four-wheel drive.

526
01:14:49,153 --> 01:14:51,849
You gonna write me up
for improperly secured load?

527
01:14:51,922 --> 01:14:53,822
You get your ass outta here.

528
01:14:56,927 --> 01:15:00,226
- [Clattering]
- [Wells] Buenos dias.

529
01:15:00,297 --> 01:15:03,892
I'm guessing this isn't the future
you had pictured for yourself

530
01:15:03,968 --> 01:15:06,562
when you first clapped eyes
on that money.

531
01:15:07,004 --> 01:15:09,768
Don't worry.
I'm not the man who's after you.

532
01:15:11,175 --> 01:15:13,075
I know that.

533
01:15:14,445 --> 01:15:15,571
I've seen him.

534
01:15:15,646 --> 01:15:18,979
You've seen him?
And you're not dead?

535
01:15:19,850 --> 01:15:21,215
Huh.

536
01:15:21,285 --> 01:15:24,049
What's this guy supposed to be,
the ultimate badass?

537
01:15:24,121 --> 01:15:26,214
Don't think that's how I'd describe him.

538
01:15:26,290 --> 01:15:28,155
How would you describe him?

539
01:15:28,225 --> 01:15:32,423
I guess I'd say
he doesn't have a sense of humor.

540
01:15:32,496 --> 01:15:35,056
- His name's Chigurh.
- Sugar?

541
01:15:35,132 --> 01:15:37,600
Chigurh. Anton Chigurh.

542
01:15:37,668 --> 01:15:41,001
- You know how he found you?
- Yeah, I know how he found me.

543
01:15:41,071 --> 01:15:44,268
- It's called a transponder.
- I know what it's called.

544
01:15:44,341 --> 01:15:47,037
- He won't find me again.
- Not that way.

545
01:15:47,111 --> 01:15:49,944
- Not any way.
- Took me about three hours.

546
01:15:50,014 --> 01:15:54,678
- Yeah, well, I been immobile.
- No. You don't understand.

547
01:15:56,987 --> 01:16:00,286
- What do you do?
- I'm retired.

548
01:16:00,357 --> 01:16:03,121
- What did you do?
- Welder.

549
01:16:03,193 --> 01:16:05,457
- Acetylene? Mig? Tig?
- Any of it.

550
01:16:05,529 --> 01:16:08,054
- If it can be welded, I can weld it.
- Cast iron?

551
01:16:08,132 --> 01:16:09,929
- Yeah.
- I don't mean braze.

552
01:16:10,000 --> 01:16:11,934
- I didn't say braze.
- Pot metal?

553
01:16:12,002 --> 01:16:13,469
What did I say?

554
01:16:15,039 --> 01:16:19,738
- Were you in 'Nam?
- Yeah, I was 'Nam.

555
01:16:21,612 --> 01:16:23,603
So was I.

556
01:16:23,681 --> 01:16:26,013
So, what does that make me, your buddy?

557
01:16:27,618 --> 01:16:32,817
Look, you gotta give me this money.
I got no other reason to protect you.

558
01:16:33,757 --> 01:16:36,590
[Groans] It's too late. I spent it.

559
01:16:37,594 --> 01:16:39,960
A million and a half
on whores and whiskey,

560
01:16:40,030 --> 01:16:42,794
and the rest of it,
I just sorta blew it in.

561
01:16:44,668 --> 01:16:47,136
How do you know he's not
on his way to Odessa?

562
01:16:51,341 --> 01:16:55,243
- Why would he go to Odessa?
- Kill your wife.

563
01:17:02,553 --> 01:17:06,956
Maybe he's the one
that needs to be worried... about me.

564
01:17:07,024 --> 01:17:08,685
He isn't.

565
01:17:08,759 --> 01:17:12,388
Yeah. You're not cut out for this.

566
01:17:12,463 --> 01:17:15,955
You're just a guy
who happened to find those vehicles.

567
01:17:21,805 --> 01:17:24,069
I'm across the river at the Hotel Eagle.

568
01:17:25,109 --> 01:17:27,577
Carson Wells.

569
01:17:27,644 --> 01:17:29,635
Call me when you've had enough.

570
01:17:29,713 --> 01:17:32,682
I can even let you keep
a little of the money.

571
01:17:32,750 --> 01:17:36,151
If I was into cutting deals,
why wouldn't I deal with Sugar?

572
01:17:36,220 --> 01:17:40,657
No, no, you don't understand.
You can't make a deal with him.

573
01:17:40,724 --> 01:17:45,525
Even if you gave him the money, he'd
still kill you for inconveniencing him.

574
01:17:45,596 --> 01:17:47,791
He's a peculiar man.

575
01:17:47,865 --> 01:17:52,962
Might even say he has principles,
principles that transcend money or drugs

576
01:17:53,036 --> 01:17:56,699
or anything like that. Not like you.

577
01:17:57,574 --> 01:17:59,974
Yeah. He's not even like me.

578
01:18:00,043 --> 01:18:03,638
No, he don't talk as much as you.
I give him points for that.

579
01:18:06,383 --> 01:18:08,647
Carla Jean, thank you for coming.

580
01:18:08,719 --> 01:18:10,482
Don't know why I did.

581
01:18:10,554 --> 01:18:13,489
I told you, I don't know where he is.

582
01:18:13,557 --> 01:18:15,889
- You haven't heard from him?
- No, I ain't.

583
01:18:15,959 --> 01:18:17,756
- Nothing?
- Not word one.

584
01:18:17,828 --> 01:18:22,026
- Would you tell me if you had?
- Well, I don't know.

585
01:18:22,099 --> 01:18:26,934
- He don't need any trouble from you.
- It ain't me he's in trouble with.

586
01:18:27,004 --> 01:18:30,599
- Who's he in trouble with then?
- Some pretty bad people.

587
01:18:30,674 --> 01:18:34,405
These people will kill him, Carla Jean.
They won't quit.

588
01:18:34,478 --> 01:18:37,504
He won't neither. He never has.

589
01:18:38,248 --> 01:18:41,649
- He can take all comers.
- [Sighs]

590
01:18:43,220 --> 01:18:46,849
You know Charlie Walser's
got that place out east of Sanderson?

591
01:18:48,659 --> 01:18:52,720
You know how he used to slaughter
beeves, hit 'em right there with a maul,

592
01:18:52,796 --> 01:18:54,627
truss 'em and slit their throats?

593
01:18:54,698 --> 01:18:57,861
Charlie's got one all trussed up,
all set to drain him

594
01:18:57,935 --> 01:19:00,233
and the beef comes to,
thrashing around.

595
01:19:01,171 --> 01:19:03,833
Six hundred pounds
of very pissed off livestock.

596
01:19:03,907 --> 01:19:06,501
You'll excuse the... Well...

597
01:19:06,577 --> 01:19:09,137
Charlie grabs a gun,
shoot the thing in the head,

598
01:19:09,213 --> 01:19:12,239
but with the swinging and thrashing,
it's a glance shot.

599
01:19:12,316 --> 01:19:14,944
Ricochets, hits Charlie in the shoulder.

600
01:19:15,018 --> 01:19:18,647
You go see Charlie, he still can't
pick up his right hand for his hat.

601
01:19:18,722 --> 01:19:22,954
The point being, that even in
the contest between man and steer,

602
01:19:23,026 --> 01:19:24,823
the issue is not certain.

603
01:19:32,502 --> 01:19:37,166
When Llewelyn calls,
just tell him I can make him safe.

604
01:19:41,011 --> 01:19:45,744
[Exhales] 'Course, they slaughter
steers a lot different these days.

605
01:19:45,816 --> 01:19:49,912
Use a air gun. Shoots out a little rod
about that far under the brain.

606
01:19:49,987 --> 01:19:53,115
Sucks right back in.
Animal never knows what hit him.

607
01:19:56,093 --> 01:19:59,859
- Why are you telling me that, sheriff?
- I don't know.

608
01:20:03,467 --> 01:20:05,230
My mind wanders.

609
01:21:28,452 --> 01:21:30,647
Hello, Carson.

610
01:21:32,956 --> 01:21:34,548
Let's go to your room.

611
01:21:46,870 --> 01:21:49,464
You don't have to do this.

612
01:21:49,539 --> 01:21:53,566
I'm a day trader.
I could just go home.

613
01:21:55,512 --> 01:21:58,379
- You could.
- Make it worth your while,

614
01:21:58,448 --> 01:22:02,646
take you to an ATM.
There's 14 grand in it.

615
01:22:02,719 --> 01:22:05,051
And everybody just walks away.

616
01:22:06,390 --> 01:22:08,483
An ATM.

617
01:22:12,496 --> 01:22:15,590
I know where the satchel is.

618
01:22:15,665 --> 01:22:17,929
If you knew, you would have it with you.

619
01:22:18,001 --> 01:22:21,903
I can find it from the river bank.
I know where it is.

620
01:22:23,440 --> 01:22:26,375
- I know something better.
- What's that?

621
01:22:26,443 --> 01:22:27,933
Where it's going to be.

622
01:22:29,579 --> 01:22:33,106
- Where's that?
- It will be brought to me

623
01:22:33,183 --> 01:22:35,674
and placed at my feet.

624
01:22:37,554 --> 01:22:40,489
You don't know to a certainty.

625
01:22:40,557 --> 01:22:44,516
- In 20 minutes it could be here.
- I do know to a certainty.

626
01:22:44,594 --> 01:22:47,290
And you know what's
gonna happen now, Carson?

627
01:22:48,365 --> 01:22:52,995
You should admit your situation.
There would be more dignity in it.

628
01:22:58,141 --> 01:23:00,974
- You go to hell.
- [Chuckles]

629
01:23:01,812 --> 01:23:05,077
All right.
Let me ask you something.

630
01:23:06,883 --> 01:23:11,650
If the rule you followed
brought you to this,

631
01:23:11,721 --> 01:23:14,315
of what use was the rule?

632
01:23:19,896 --> 01:23:23,798
Do you have any idea how crazy you are?

633
01:23:24,968 --> 01:23:27,334
You mean the nature
of this conversation?

634
01:23:27,404 --> 01:23:29,565
I mean the nature of you.

635
01:23:33,877 --> 01:23:39,713
You... You can have the money, Anton.

636
01:23:42,519 --> 01:23:45,818
[Phone rings]

637
01:23:57,367 --> 01:24:01,133
- [Wells grunts]
- [Phone continues to ring]

638
01:24:19,789 --> 01:24:22,155
- [Llewelyn] Hello?
- Yes?

639
01:24:23,827 --> 01:24:27,058
Is, uh, Carson Wells there?

640
01:24:29,299 --> 01:24:31,790
Not in the sense that you mean.

641
01:24:33,970 --> 01:24:37,963
- You need to come see me.
- Who is this?

642
01:24:38,041 --> 01:24:41,033
You know who it is.

643
01:24:41,111 --> 01:24:44,274
- You need to talk to me.
- I don't need to talk to you.

644
01:24:44,347 --> 01:24:46,679
I think you do.

645
01:24:46,750 --> 01:24:48,479
Do you know where I'm going?

646
01:24:48,552 --> 01:24:51,146
Why would I care where you're going?

647
01:24:51,221 --> 01:24:53,883
I know where you are.

648
01:24:53,957 --> 01:24:56,152
Yeah, where am I?

649
01:24:56,226 --> 01:25:00,492
You're in the hospital across the river,
but that's not where I'm going.

650
01:25:01,398 --> 01:25:03,491
Do you know where I'm going?

651
01:25:05,335 --> 01:25:09,362
- Yeah, I know where you're going.
- All right.

652
01:25:09,439 --> 01:25:10,997
You know she won't be there.

653
01:25:11,074 --> 01:25:13,542
It doesn't make any difference
where she is.

654
01:25:13,610 --> 01:25:15,771
So, what're you going up there for?

655
01:25:16,713 --> 01:25:18,647
[Sighs]

656
01:25:19,416 --> 01:25:21,941
You know how this is gonna turn out,
don't you?

657
01:25:23,920 --> 01:25:25,444
Nope.

658
01:25:25,522 --> 01:25:29,788
I think you do.
So this is what I'll offer.

659
01:25:30,794 --> 01:25:33,354
You bring me the money,
and I'll let her go.

660
01:25:33,430 --> 01:25:38,094
Otherwise, she's accountable,
the same as you.

661
01:25:40,203 --> 01:25:42,603
That's the best deal you're gonna get.

662
01:25:42,672 --> 01:25:46,608
I won't tell you you can save yourself,
because you can't.

663
01:25:49,879 --> 01:25:53,315
Yeah, I'm gonna bring you something,
all right.

664
01:25:53,383 --> 01:25:55,544
Decided to make you a project of mine.

665
01:25:55,619 --> 01:25:58,281
You ain't gonna have
to come look for me at all.

666
01:26:02,592 --> 01:26:05,152
- The motel in Del Rio?
- Yes, sir.

667
01:26:05,228 --> 01:26:06,991
None of the three had ID on 'em,

668
01:26:07,063 --> 01:26:10,829
but they're tellin' me that all three
is Mexican... was Mexicans.

669
01:26:10,900 --> 01:26:15,769
There's a question,
whether they stopped being and when.

670
01:26:17,774 --> 01:26:19,708
Yes, sir.

671
01:26:19,776 --> 01:26:22,370
Wendell, did you inquire
about the lock cylinder?

672
01:26:22,445 --> 01:26:25,073
- Yes, sir. It was punched out.
- OK.

673
01:26:25,915 --> 01:26:28,907
- Wanna drive out there?
- No, that's all I had to look for,

674
01:26:28,985 --> 01:26:31,510
it sound like these boys
died of natural causes.

675
01:26:31,588 --> 01:26:35,524
- How's that, sheriff?
- Natural to the line of work they in.

676
01:26:35,592 --> 01:26:38,459
- Yes, sir.
- My God, Wendell, it's all-out war.

677
01:26:38,528 --> 01:26:42,521
I can't think of any other word for it.
Who are these people?

678
01:26:42,599 --> 01:26:46,057
Here, last week they found
this couple out in California.

679
01:26:46,136 --> 01:26:49,867
They rent out rooms to old people,
kill 'em, bury 'em in the yard,

680
01:26:49,939 --> 01:26:52,237
cash their Social Security checks.

681
01:26:53,276 --> 01:26:56,439
Oh, they'd torture 'em first.
I don't know why.

682
01:26:57,614 --> 01:26:59,741
Maybe their television set was broke.

683
01:26:59,816 --> 01:27:03,183
And this went on until, here, I quote,

684
01:27:03,253 --> 01:27:07,155
"Neighbors were alerted
when a man ran from the premises

685
01:27:07,223 --> 01:27:09,214
wearing only a dog collar."

686
01:27:10,960 --> 01:27:13,952
You can't make up such
a thing as that. I dare you to try.

687
01:27:14,030 --> 01:27:17,727
But that's what it took, you notice,
to get somebody's attention.

688
01:27:17,801 --> 01:27:20,531
Digging graves in the backyard
didn't bring any.

689
01:27:20,603 --> 01:27:22,298
[Suppresses laughter]

690
01:27:27,243 --> 01:27:30,974
Well, that's all right.
I laugh myself sometimes.

691
01:27:33,083 --> 01:27:35,108
Ain't a whole lot else you can do.

692
01:27:37,721 --> 01:27:39,712
Tell me something.

693
01:27:39,789 --> 01:27:42,781
Who gets through this gate
into the United States?

694
01:27:44,561 --> 01:27:47,086
I don't know. American citizens?

695
01:27:47,163 --> 01:27:50,064
Some American citizens.
Who do you think decides?

696
01:27:50,133 --> 01:27:52,124
- You do, I reckon.
- That is correct.

697
01:27:52,202 --> 01:27:54,500
- How do I decide?
- I don't know.

698
01:27:54,571 --> 01:27:58,507
I ask questions. If I get sensible
answers, then they get to go to America.

699
01:27:58,575 --> 01:27:59,940
If I don't, they don't.

700
01:28:00,009 --> 01:28:02,068
- Anything you don't understand?
- No.

701
01:28:02,145 --> 01:28:04,807
I ask again, how you come
to be here with no clothes?

702
01:28:06,216 --> 01:28:07,774
Well, I got an overcoat on.

703
01:28:09,052 --> 01:28:10,986
- Are you jacking with me?
- No, sir.

704
01:28:11,054 --> 01:28:13,113
- Don't jack with me.
- Yes, sir.

705
01:28:13,723 --> 01:28:15,918
- You in the service?
- No, I'm a veteran.

706
01:28:15,992 --> 01:28:18,460
- 'Nam?
- Yes, sir, two tours.

707
01:28:18,528 --> 01:28:21,122
- What outfit?
- Twelfth Infantry Battalion,

708
01:28:21,197 --> 01:28:24,792
August 7th, 1966, July 2nd, 1968.

709
01:28:27,470 --> 01:28:30,439
- Wilson!
- Yes, sir!

710
01:28:30,507 --> 01:28:34,068
Get someone to help this man.
He needs to get into town.

711
01:28:43,086 --> 01:28:46,522
- How those Larry's holdin' up?
- Good, good.

712
01:28:46,589 --> 01:28:49,149
- I need everything else.
- OK.

713
01:28:49,726 --> 01:28:52,820
Have a lot of people come
in here without any clothes on?

714
01:28:52,896 --> 01:28:55,228
No, sir. It's unusual.

715
01:29:08,211 --> 01:29:11,703
- [Woman] She don't want to talk to you.
- Yes, she does. Put her on.

716
01:29:11,781 --> 01:29:14,306
- Do you know what time it is?
- I don't care,

717
01:29:14,384 --> 01:29:16,682
and don't you hang up this phone.

718
01:29:16,753 --> 01:29:19,916
Discombobulated
by a no-account son-in-law.

719
01:29:21,090 --> 01:29:22,955
- Llewelyn?
- Hey, you.

720
01:29:23,026 --> 01:29:26,189
- What should I do?
- You know what's going on?

721
01:29:26,262 --> 01:29:29,993
I don't know. I had the sheriff here
from Terrell County. He...

722
01:29:30,066 --> 01:29:33,365
- What'd you tell him?
- What did I know to tell him?

723
01:29:34,070 --> 01:29:37,233
- You're hurt, ain't you?
- [Scoffs]

724
01:29:37,307 --> 01:29:39,571
What makes you say that?

725
01:29:39,642 --> 01:29:43,203
- I can hear it in your voice.
- [Woman] It's falseness in his voice!

726
01:29:43,279 --> 01:29:46,874
Look, I want you to meet me at
the Desert Sands Motel in El Paso.

727
01:29:46,950 --> 01:29:49,418
I'm gonna give you the money,
put you on a plane.

728
01:29:49,486 --> 01:29:51,920
Llewelyn, I ain't gonna
leave you in the lurch.

729
01:29:51,988 --> 01:29:55,185
This works better. With you
gone and I don't have the money,

730
01:29:55,258 --> 01:29:58,056
he can't touch me,
but I can sure touch him.

731
01:29:58,127 --> 01:30:00,789
And after I find him,
I'll come and join you.

732
01:30:00,864 --> 01:30:04,322
Find who?
What am I supposed to do with mother?

733
01:30:04,400 --> 01:30:06,300
- She'll be all right.
- All right?

734
01:30:06,369 --> 01:30:08,894
[Woman] Be all right? I got the cancer!

735
01:30:08,972 --> 01:30:10,940
Ain't nobody gonna bother her.

736
01:30:33,730 --> 01:30:36,756
[Man gurgles]

737
01:30:42,572 --> 01:30:45,040
- [Gurgling continues]
- Hmm.

738
01:30:45,508 --> 01:30:46,998
Who are you?

739
01:30:51,381 --> 01:30:53,212
- Me?
- Yes.

740
01:30:53,283 --> 01:30:55,478
Nobody.

741
01:30:55,552 --> 01:30:57,247
Accounting.

742
01:30:58,688 --> 01:31:01,156
He gave the Mexicans a receiver.

743
01:31:03,493 --> 01:31:05,461
He feels...

744
01:31:05,528 --> 01:31:09,157
He felt that the more people looking...

745
01:31:09,232 --> 01:31:14,169
That's foolish.
You pick the one right tool.

746
01:31:14,237 --> 01:31:15,636
I see.

747
01:31:18,541 --> 01:31:20,168
Are you going to shoot me?

748
01:31:26,416 --> 01:31:28,213
That depends.

749
01:31:30,587 --> 01:31:32,179
Do you see me?

750
01:31:35,325 --> 01:31:37,919
[Woman] I always seen this
is what it'd come to.

751
01:31:37,994 --> 01:31:42,294
- Three years ago, I pre-visioned it.
- It ain't three years we been married.

752
01:31:42,365 --> 01:31:46,563
Three years ago I said
them very words, "no" and "good."

753
01:31:46,636 --> 01:31:50,299
Here we are, 90 degree heat,

754
01:31:50,373 --> 01:31:55,777
I got the cancer and look at this,
not even a home to go to.

755
01:31:56,479 --> 01:31:59,277
We're going to El Paso, Texas.

756
01:31:59,349 --> 01:32:02,512
You know how may people
I know in El Paso, Texas?

757
01:32:02,585 --> 01:32:05,520
- No, ma'am.
- That's how many.

758
01:32:07,490 --> 01:32:09,287
[Man speaks Spanish]

759
01:32:10,960 --> 01:32:12,484
[Speaks Spanish]

760
01:32:14,464 --> 01:32:17,695
- I didn't see my prednisone.
- I put it in, Mama.

761
01:32:17,767 --> 01:32:21,498
- Well, I didn't see it.
- Well, I put it in that one.

762
01:32:21,571 --> 01:32:25,405
You just set there. I'll get tickets
and a cart for the bags.

763
01:32:25,475 --> 01:32:27,636
You need help with the bags, madam?

764
01:32:27,710 --> 01:32:31,612
Well, thank God, there is
one gentleman left in west Texas.

765
01:32:31,681 --> 01:32:34,445
Yes, thank you.
I am old and I am not well!

766
01:32:34,517 --> 01:32:39,216
- Which bus are you taking?
- Going to El Paso, don't ask me why.

767
01:32:39,288 --> 01:32:42,223
It's not often you see
a Mexican in a suit.

768
01:32:42,291 --> 01:32:44,851
You go to El Paso? I know it.
Where are you staying?

769
01:32:44,927 --> 01:32:46,224
Oh...

770
01:32:46,295 --> 01:32:48,058
[sheriff] Carla Jean, how are you?

771
01:32:48,131 --> 01:32:52,124
Sheriff, was that a true story
about Charlie Walser?

772
01:32:52,201 --> 01:32:54,692
Who's Charlie Walser? Uh! Oh...

773
01:32:54,771 --> 01:32:56,864
[chuckles] Um...

774
01:32:56,939 --> 01:32:59,499
True story.
I couldn't swear to every detail,

775
01:32:59,575 --> 01:33:03,102
- but it's certainly true it is a story.
- Yeah, right.

776
01:33:03,179 --> 01:33:07,013
Sheriff, can you give me
your word on something?

777
01:33:07,083 --> 01:33:09,017
Yes, ma'am.

778
01:33:09,085 --> 01:33:13,715
If I tell you where Llewelyn's headed,
you promise it'll be just you

779
01:33:13,790 --> 01:33:16,953
who goes and talks with him?
You and nobody else.

780
01:33:17,026 --> 01:33:20,962
- Yes, ma'am, I do.
- Llewelyn would never ask for help.

781
01:33:21,030 --> 01:33:23,897
He never thinks he needs any.

782
01:33:23,966 --> 01:33:27,868
Carla Jean, I will not harm your man.

783
01:33:27,937 --> 01:33:31,373
And he needs help,
whether he knows it or not.

784
01:33:40,316 --> 01:33:42,409
What's the problem there, neighbor?

785
01:33:42,485 --> 01:33:46,854
Yep, that'll suck some power, over time.

786
01:33:48,658 --> 01:33:52,424
- You from around here?
- Yeah, Alpine, born and bred.

787
01:33:52,495 --> 01:33:56,556
- Here you go.
- What airport would you use?

788
01:33:56,632 --> 01:34:00,830
- Well, airport or airstrip?
- Airport.

789
01:34:00,903 --> 01:34:04,669
- Well, where you goin'?
- I don't know.

790
01:34:04,741 --> 01:34:09,201
Just lightin' out for the territories,
huh? [laughs] Brother, I been there.

791
01:34:09,746 --> 01:34:12,977
Well, there's airstrips.

792
01:34:13,049 --> 01:34:15,381
The airport is El Paso.

793
01:34:15,451 --> 01:34:17,248
If you want someplace specific,

794
01:34:17,320 --> 01:34:21,279
you might could be better off just
driving to Dallas, not have to connect.

795
01:34:21,357 --> 01:34:23,552
You gonna clamp them, buddy?

796
01:34:23,626 --> 01:34:26,527
Can you get those chicken crates
out of the bed?

797
01:34:26,596 --> 01:34:29,463
What are you talking about?

798
01:34:45,381 --> 01:34:49,374
Hey, Mr. Sportin' Goods.

799
01:34:49,452 --> 01:34:53,411
- Hey, yourself.
- You a sport?

800
01:34:53,489 --> 01:34:55,889
Yeah, that's me.

801
01:34:55,958 --> 01:34:58,756
I got beers in my room.

802
01:34:58,828 --> 01:35:02,730
- Oh, uh, I'm... I'm waiting on my wife.
- Oh.

803
01:35:02,799 --> 01:35:05,632
That's who you keep
looking out the window for?

804
01:35:05,701 --> 01:35:08,329
- Well, half.
- What else then?

805
01:35:08,404 --> 01:35:13,239
- Just looking for what's comin'.
- [Woman] Yeah.

806
01:35:13,309 --> 01:35:16,005
But no one ever sees that.

807
01:35:16,078 --> 01:35:19,377
Beer! That's what's comin'.

808
01:35:19,448 --> 01:35:22,246
I'll bring the ice chest out.
You can stay married.

809
01:35:22,318 --> 01:35:24,809
No, ma'am, I know what beer leads to.

810
01:35:24,887 --> 01:35:26,582
[Laughs]

811
01:35:26,656 --> 01:35:28,783
Beer leads to more beer.

812
01:35:34,463 --> 01:35:38,490
[Rapid gunfire]

813
01:35:38,568 --> 01:35:41,696
- [Men shouting in Spanish]
- [Tires squealing]

814
01:35:56,519 --> 01:35:59,010
[Woman sobbing]

815
01:35:59,088 --> 01:36:01,022
[Woman screams]

816
01:36:13,603 --> 01:36:15,628
[Women crying]

817
01:36:15,705 --> 01:36:17,172
You all right?

818
01:36:22,445 --> 01:36:24,310
Call the police.

819
01:36:24,380 --> 01:36:27,042
[Speaks Spanish]

820
01:36:30,152 --> 01:36:32,416
Your local law enforcement.

821
01:36:33,756 --> 01:36:35,747
I'm not on their radio.

822
01:36:35,825 --> 01:36:38,555
[Man] I met his daughter at the...

823
01:37:26,008 --> 01:37:29,205
[man] Buy you a cup of coffee
'fore you drive home?

824
01:37:29,278 --> 01:37:32,839
- No money in his room there?
- Couple hundred, on his person.

825
01:37:32,915 --> 01:37:35,247
Those hombres
would have taken the stash.

826
01:37:35,318 --> 01:37:37,878
I suppose so.

827
01:37:37,954 --> 01:37:40,115
Though they was leavin' in a hurry.

828
01:37:40,189 --> 01:37:45,388
It's all the goddamn money, Ed Tom.
Money and the drugs.

829
01:37:46,562 --> 01:37:49,224
It's just goddamn beyond everything.

830
01:37:49,966 --> 01:37:53,197
What's it mean? What's it leading to?

831
01:37:53,269 --> 01:37:56,329
You know,
if you'd have told me 20 years ago

832
01:37:56,405 --> 01:37:59,533
I'd see children walking
the streets of our Texas towns

833
01:37:59,608 --> 01:38:02,736
with green hair, bones in their noses,

834
01:38:02,812 --> 01:38:05,372
I just flat-out
wouldn't have believed you.

835
01:38:05,448 --> 01:38:07,006
Signs and wonders.

836
01:38:07,083 --> 01:38:11,645
But I think once you quit hearing "sir"
and "ma'am," the rest is soon to foller.

837
01:38:11,721 --> 01:38:13,450
- Oh, it's the tide.
- Yeah.

838
01:38:13,522 --> 01:38:17,356
It's the dismal tide.
It is not the one thing.

839
01:38:17,426 --> 01:38:19,018
Not the one thing.

840
01:38:19,095 --> 01:38:21,962
Yeah, well,
none of that explains your man, though.

841
01:38:22,031 --> 01:38:25,159
Well, he's just a goddamn
homicidal lunatic, Ed Tom.

842
01:38:25,234 --> 01:38:28,601
- I'm not sure he's a lunatic.
- Well, what would you call him?

843
01:38:28,671 --> 01:38:32,038
Well, sometimes
I think he's pretty much a ghost.

844
01:38:32,108 --> 01:38:34,042
- He's real, all right.
- Oh, yeah?

845
01:38:34,110 --> 01:38:37,876
Yeah. All of that over at
the Eagle Hotel? Just beyond everything.

846
01:38:37,947 --> 01:38:40,507
Yeah. Got some hard bark on him.

847
01:38:40,583 --> 01:38:43,484
Why... Well, that don't hardly say it.

848
01:38:44,687 --> 01:38:46,518
He shoots a desk clerk one day,

849
01:38:46,589 --> 01:38:51,083
walks right back in the next
and shoots a retired Army colonel.

850
01:38:51,160 --> 01:38:52,650
It's hard to believe.

851
01:38:52,728 --> 01:38:55,993
Just strolls right back
into a crime scene.

852
01:38:56,065 --> 01:39:01,435
Now, who'd do such a thing?
How do you defend against it?

853
01:39:01,504 --> 01:39:04,496
Well, good trip, Ed Tom.

854
01:39:04,573 --> 01:39:06,973
Sorry we couldn't help your boy.

855
01:39:32,601 --> 01:39:35,001
[Engine shuts off]

856
01:39:42,344 --> 01:39:44,312
[Sighs]

857
01:41:49,071 --> 01:41:51,266
[Sighs]

858
01:41:53,175 --> 01:41:56,633
[Airplane flies overheard]

859
01:42:32,715 --> 01:42:35,411
[Cats meow]

860
01:42:39,088 --> 01:42:42,319
- [Man] In back!
- How'd you know I was here?

861
01:42:42,391 --> 01:42:45,485
Who else'd be driving up in your truck?

862
01:42:45,561 --> 01:42:47,119
- You heard it?
- How's that?

863
01:42:47,196 --> 01:42:51,394
Did you hear my?
You're having fun with me.

864
01:42:51,467 --> 01:42:55,836
What give you that idea?
I seen one of the cats heard it.

865
01:42:55,904 --> 01:43:00,705
- How'd you know it was my truck?
- I deduced it when you walked in.

866
01:43:00,776 --> 01:43:02,937
How many of them things you got now?

867
01:43:03,012 --> 01:43:05,537
Cats? Oh, I don't know, several.

868
01:43:05,614 --> 01:43:08,913
Well, it depends
on what you mean by "got."

869
01:43:08,984 --> 01:43:13,216
Some of 'em are half wild,
some of 'em are just outlaws.

870
01:43:16,292 --> 01:43:20,023
- How you been, Ellis?
- You're looking at it.

871
01:43:21,397 --> 01:43:24,696
I got to say, you're looking older.

872
01:43:24,767 --> 01:43:26,291
I am older.

873
01:43:27,436 --> 01:43:29,904
Got a letter from your wife.

874
01:43:29,972 --> 01:43:34,409
She writes me pretty regular,
keeps me up on the family news.

875
01:43:34,476 --> 01:43:38,173
- Didn't know there was any.
- Told me you're quittin'.

876
01:43:38,247 --> 01:43:40,647
[Inhales, exhales deeply]

877
01:43:41,250 --> 01:43:44,151
- You want a cup?
- Appreciate it.

878
01:43:51,660 --> 01:43:53,491
How fresh is that coffee?

879
01:43:53,562 --> 01:43:59,296
I generally make a fresh pot every week,
even if there's some left over.

880
01:44:01,236 --> 01:44:03,568
That man that shot you died in prison?

881
01:44:05,941 --> 01:44:08,171
Angola. Yeah.

882
01:44:08,243 --> 01:44:11,041
What'd you done he'd a been released?

883
01:44:11,113 --> 01:44:14,014
Oh, I don't know. Nothing.

884
01:44:14,083 --> 01:44:15,710
Wouldn't be no point in it.

885
01:44:15,784 --> 01:44:19,049
Kindly surprised to hear you say that.

886
01:44:19,121 --> 01:44:22,852
Well, all the time
you spend trying to get back

887
01:44:22,925 --> 01:44:26,588
what's been took from you,
more is going out the door.

888
01:44:26,662 --> 01:44:29,631
After a while, you have
to try to get a tourniquet on it.

889
01:44:30,699 --> 01:44:34,829
Your granddad never asked me
to sign on as a deputy.

890
01:44:37,639 --> 01:44:41,666
Loretta tells me you're quittin'.
How come you doing that?

891
01:44:41,744 --> 01:44:44,042
I don't know.

892
01:44:46,749 --> 01:44:48,410
I feel overmatched.

893
01:44:52,988 --> 01:44:55,582
I always figured when I got older,

894
01:44:55,657 --> 01:45:00,560
God would... sort of
come into my life somehow.

895
01:45:03,565 --> 01:45:04,623
And He didn't.

896
01:45:08,504 --> 01:45:10,131
And I don't blame Him.

897
01:45:12,508 --> 01:45:15,443
If I was Him, I'd have
the same opinion of me that He does.

898
01:45:16,078 --> 01:45:17,875
You don't know what He thinks.

899
01:45:32,094 --> 01:45:35,825
I sent Uncle Mac's thumbbuster and badge
over to the Rangers,

900
01:45:35,898 --> 01:45:38,526
put it in their museum.

901
01:45:38,600 --> 01:45:42,229
Your daddy ever tell you
how Uncle Mac come to his reward?

902
01:45:43,305 --> 01:45:46,866
Gunned down on his own porch
over in Hudspeth County.

903
01:45:49,111 --> 01:45:53,980
Seven or eight of 'em come up there,
all wanting this, wanting that.

904
01:45:54,049 --> 01:45:57,018
Uncle Mac went back in the house
to get the shotgun.

905
01:45:57,085 --> 01:46:00,714
Well, they was ahead of him.

906
01:46:00,789 --> 01:46:03,519
Shot him in his doorway.

907
01:46:03,592 --> 01:46:06,186
Aunt Ella come out,
tried to stop the bleeding.

908
01:46:06,261 --> 01:46:10,197
Uncle Mac all the while
trying to get that shotgun.

909
01:46:10,265 --> 01:46:14,702
They just sat there on their horses,
watching him die.

910
01:46:15,804 --> 01:46:19,968
After a while, one of 'em said
something in Indian and they turned.

911
01:46:20,876 --> 01:46:22,673
Left out.

912
01:46:24,847 --> 01:46:28,339
Uncle Mac knew the score,
even if Aunt Ella didn't.

913
01:46:28,417 --> 01:46:31,545
Shot through the left lung.

914
01:46:31,620 --> 01:46:36,148
And that was that... as they say.

915
01:46:39,428 --> 01:46:40,725
When'd he die?

916
01:46:41,997 --> 01:46:45,364
Nineteen zero and, uh... nine?

917
01:46:45,434 --> 01:46:49,268
No, I mean,
was it right away or in the night or...

918
01:46:49,338 --> 01:46:53,035
- When was it?
- I believe it was that night.

919
01:46:53,108 --> 01:46:58,136
She buried him the next morning.
Digging in that hard old caliche.

920
01:47:01,083 --> 01:47:03,847
What you got ain't nothin' new.

921
01:47:04,987 --> 01:47:07,683
This country is hard on people.

922
01:47:11,426 --> 01:47:13,553
You can't stop what's coming.

923
01:47:15,364 --> 01:47:17,423
It ain't all waitin' on you.

924
01:47:20,269 --> 01:47:22,134
That's vanity.

925
01:48:23,065 --> 01:48:25,431
I knew this wasn't done with.

926
01:48:28,270 --> 01:48:30,170
I ain't got the money.

927
01:48:32,207 --> 01:48:36,439
What little I had is long gone
and there's bills aplenty to pay yet.

928
01:48:38,380 --> 01:48:42,908
I buried my mother today.
Ain't paid for that neither.

929
01:48:43,919 --> 01:48:45,784
I wouldn't worry about it.

930
01:48:48,423 --> 01:48:49,981
I need to sit down.

931
01:49:05,273 --> 01:49:07,366
You got no cause to hurt me.

932
01:49:07,442 --> 01:49:11,276
No. But I gave my word.

933
01:49:12,714 --> 01:49:16,343
- You gave your word?
- To your husband.

934
01:49:19,354 --> 01:49:22,118
That don't make sense.

935
01:49:22,190 --> 01:49:25,421
You gave your word
to my husband to kill me?

936
01:49:25,494 --> 01:49:28,986
Your husband had
the opportunity to save you.

937
01:49:29,064 --> 01:49:31,931
Instead, he used you
to try to save himself.

938
01:49:36,938 --> 01:49:38,929
Not like that.

939
01:49:40,509 --> 01:49:42,340
Not like you say.

940
01:49:48,417 --> 01:49:50,214
You don't have to do this.

941
01:49:50,285 --> 01:49:52,549
People always say the same thing.

942
01:49:54,289 --> 01:49:55,551
What do they say?

943
01:49:56,658 --> 01:49:58,888
They say, "You don't have to do this."

944
01:50:00,062 --> 01:50:05,125
- You don't.
- OK.

945
01:50:10,238 --> 01:50:12,229
This is the best I can do.

946
01:50:13,341 --> 01:50:14,330
Call it.

947
01:50:18,847 --> 01:50:22,442
I knowed you was crazy
when I saw you sitting there.

948
01:50:24,853 --> 01:50:27,481
I knowed exactly
what was in store for me.

949
01:50:27,556 --> 01:50:31,890
- Call it.
- No.

950
01:50:31,960 --> 01:50:34,485
I ain't gonna call it.

951
01:50:37,866 --> 01:50:39,265
Call it.

952
01:50:39,334 --> 01:50:42,394
The coin don't have no say.

953
01:50:42,471 --> 01:50:44,336
It's just you.

954
01:50:46,875 --> 01:50:49,969
Well, I got here
the same way the coin did.

955
01:50:52,748 --> 01:50:54,978
[Rattling]

956
01:51:18,840 --> 01:51:21,434
[Boys chattering]

957
01:51:54,276 --> 01:51:57,803
- [Glass clattering]
- [Groaning]

958
01:52:10,158 --> 01:52:13,719
[Breathing heavily]

959
01:52:28,410 --> 01:52:32,506
[Groaning]

960
01:52:32,581 --> 01:52:35,744
Mister, you got
a bone sticking out of your arm.

961
01:52:35,817 --> 01:52:38,217
I'm all right.
Let me just sit here a minute.

962
01:52:38,286 --> 01:52:41,221
There's an ambulance coming.
The man went to call.

963
01:52:41,289 --> 01:52:43,587
All right.

964
01:52:43,658 --> 01:52:47,492
Are you all right?
You got a bone sticking out your arm.

965
01:52:48,763 --> 01:52:50,731
What'll you take for the shirt?

966
01:52:50,799 --> 01:52:53,267
- [Sirens approaching]
- [Panting]

967
01:52:55,303 --> 01:52:58,272
Well, hell, mister,
I'll give you my shirt.

968
01:53:05,280 --> 01:53:08,738
Look at that fucking bone.

969
01:53:08,817 --> 01:53:09,977
Tie this for me.

970
01:53:11,086 --> 01:53:13,054
Just tie it, just tie it.

971
01:53:13,922 --> 01:53:16,584
- [Sirens approaching]
- [Exhales]

972
01:53:31,072 --> 01:53:35,099
Hell, mister.
Look, I don't mind helping someone out.

973
01:53:35,176 --> 01:53:37,667
- That's a lot of money.
- Take it.

974
01:53:37,746 --> 01:53:40,874
Take it and... you didn't see me.

975
01:53:40,949 --> 01:53:43,213
- I was already gone.
- [Sirens approaching]

976
01:53:43,285 --> 01:53:44,582
Yes, sir.

977
01:53:48,990 --> 01:53:52,517
- Part of that's mine, right?
- You still got your damn shirt.

978
01:53:52,594 --> 01:53:56,997
- Ain't even what it was for.
- Maybe. But I'm still out a shirt.

979
01:54:17,118 --> 01:54:19,746
- Maybe I'll go ridin'.
- OK.

980
01:54:19,821 --> 01:54:23,689
- What do you think?
- Well, I can't plan your day.

981
01:54:24,960 --> 01:54:28,487
- I mean, would you care to join me?
- Lord, no, I'm not retired.

982
01:54:30,532 --> 01:54:34,935
- Maybe I'll help out here then.
- Uh... better not.

983
01:54:39,240 --> 01:54:42,573
- How'd you sleep?
- I don't know. Had dreams.

984
01:54:42,644 --> 01:54:47,013
Well, you got time for 'em now.
Anything interesting?

985
01:54:47,082 --> 01:54:49,915
They always is to the party concerned.

986
01:54:52,153 --> 01:54:54,747
Ed Tom, I'll be polite.

987
01:54:57,192 --> 01:55:02,289
All right, then. Two of 'em, both
had my father in 'em. It's peculiar.

988
01:55:02,364 --> 01:55:05,356
I'm older now
than he ever was by 20 years.

989
01:55:05,433 --> 01:55:09,301
So, in a sense, he's the younger man.

990
01:55:09,371 --> 01:55:12,465
Anyway, the first one
I don't remember too well,

991
01:55:12,540 --> 01:55:15,270
but it was about meeting him in town,

992
01:55:15,343 --> 01:55:17,937
somewheres,
and he give me some money.

993
01:55:18,013 --> 01:55:19,640
I think I lost it.

994
01:55:21,449 --> 01:55:26,352
Second one, it was like we
was both back in older times.

995
01:55:26,421 --> 01:55:30,517
And I was a-horseback,
going through the mountains of a night.

996
01:55:30,592 --> 01:55:34,028
Going through this pass
in the mountains.

997
01:55:34,095 --> 01:55:37,496
It was cold,
and there was snow on the ground.

998
01:55:37,565 --> 01:55:40,033
And he rode past me and kept on going,

999
01:55:40,101 --> 01:55:42,865
never said nothing going by,
just rode on past.

1000
01:55:44,506 --> 01:55:48,306
He had his blanket wrapped around him
and his head down.

1001
01:55:50,111 --> 01:55:54,104
When he rode past,
I seen he was carrying fire in a horn,

1002
01:55:54,182 --> 01:55:56,082
the way people used to do, and I...

1003
01:55:56,151 --> 01:55:58,745
I could see the horn
from the light inside of it,

1004
01:55:58,820 --> 01:56:00,549
'bout the color of the moon.

1005
01:56:02,057 --> 01:56:04,389
And, in the dream, I knew that he was...

1006
01:56:05,260 --> 01:56:07,057
...going on ahead.

1007
01:56:07,662 --> 01:56:12,531
He was fixin' to make a fire somewhere
out there in all that dark and cold.

1008
01:56:16,137 --> 01:56:19,129
And I knew that whenever I got there,
he'd be there.

1009
01:56:23,678 --> 01:56:25,646
And then I woke up.

